🚽 Test case: `!` on a struct must be a semantic error, not a crash.
🚽
🚽 A `!` expression infers as cap whatever the operand was, which is right
🚽 for scalars but removes the protection a struct used to get for free:
🚽 `yapping("%b", -s)` is refused as "struct has no supported native ABI
🚽 representation", while `!s` looks like an ordinary cap and used to sail
🚽 through to an int evaluator with no int to read -- a null load and an
🚽 ASan DEADLYSIGNAL.
gang S { rizz a; };

skibidi main {
    gang S s;
    s.a = 1;
    yapping("%b", !s);
    bussin 0;
}
